1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,102
That was the CTO's question after a week
of reports about submodules, which are

2
00:00:09,142 --> 00:00:13,744
other repositories checked out inside
yours: an empty directory, not our ref, a

3
00:00:13,784 --> 00:00:18,209
silent downgrade, a red main. You have
spent three videos on the first half.

4
00:00:18,249 --> 00:00:22,851
You can now explain every one of those
failures as a disagreement between three

5
00:00:22,891 --> 00:00:23,262
values.

6
00:00:23,602 --> 00:00:27,188
The second half is a decision,
and it deserves a real answer.

7
00:00:27,228 --> 00:00:32,181
There are two alternatives. One of them
makes all four reports impossible, because a

8
00:00:32,221 --> 00:00:36,044
plain clone contains everything,
and pays for that in other ways.

9
00:00:36,084 --> 00:00:40,621
Hold on to that claim. The other isn't
a Git mechanism at all: it's a package

10
00:00:40,661 --> 00:00:45,019
manager, such as pip or npm, and for
most libraries it's the right answer.

11
00:00:46,859 --> 00:00:50,757
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,797 --> 00:00:56,116
Today you solve the same problem a second
time. The library textsplit goes into the

13
00:00:56,156 --> 00:01:01,409
service doc-qa again, under the same path,
as a subtree: its files become ordinary

14
00:01:01,449 --> 00:01:03,927
files in your repository's own history.

15
00:01:04,267 --> 00:01:08,913
You'll add it, see what a teammate's clone
contains, pull an upstream release into

16
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:13,713
it, change the library and the application
in one commit, and offer the library part

17
00:01:13,753 --> 00:01:18,342
back upstream. Then the decision table:
submodule, subtree, or a package manager.

18
00:01:20,182 --> 00:01:24,616
add a dependency as a subtree and show
what the superproject's history then

19
00:01:24,656 --> 00:01:25,153
contains;

20
00:01:25,493 --> 00:01:29,647
pull an upstream release into the subtree
and contribute a local change back;

21
00:01:29,987 --> 00:01:34,083
explain how git subtree split can
reproduce upstream commit IDs;

22
00:01:34,423 --> 00:01:39,119
decide between submodule, subtree and
package manager for a described dependency.

23
00:01:40,959 --> 00:01:45,636
In one sentence: 🟡 CAUTION, git subtree
add copies another project's files into a

24
00:01:45,676 --> 00:01:50,296
subdirectory of your repository as ordinary
tracked files, and records in commit

25
00:01:50,336 --> 00:01:54,839
messages where they came from, so that
later commands can merge newer upstream

26
00:01:54,879 --> 00:01:57,460
versions in or extract
your changes back out.

27
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:02,133
Now precisely. git subtree is a
script from Git's contrib/ directory.

28
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:07,013
The Homebrew Git on the course's Mac
installs it. It has no page in git help.

29
00:02:07,053 --> 00:02:12,147
Its documentation is in the Git source
tree, and git subtree -h prints the usage.

30
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:17,470
That documentation states the contrast with
submodules itself: subtrees "do not need

31
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:22,604
any special constructions (like .gitmodules
files or gitlinks) be present in your

32
00:02:22,644 --> 00:02:27,864
repository, and do not force end-users of
your repository to do anything special or

33
00:02:27,904 --> 00:02:29,956
to understand how subtrees work".

34
00:02:30,296 --> 00:02:35,455
Don't confuse the command with the subtree
merge strategy of git merge -s subtree,

35
00:02:35,495 --> 00:02:36,660
which it builds on.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,327
Inside .git: nothing special at all. The
entry at the prefix, the subdirectory you

37
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:45,993
chose, has mode 040000, a tree
like any other directory.

38
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:50,640
The files are blobs in your object
database. There's no .gitmodules, no

39
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:55,680
.git/modules, no configuration. The whole
mechanism is two trailer lines, the

40
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:00,065
labelled lines at the end of a
commit message: git-subtree-dir, and

41
00:03:00,105 --> 00:03:04,712
git-subtree-split, the upstream commit
that the content corresponds to.

42
00:03:05,052 --> 00:03:09,871
Updating. 🟡 CAUTION: git subtree pull
fetches the upstream branch, builds a squash

43
00:03:09,911 --> 00:03:14,849
commit, one commit that carries the whole
difference since the recorded split point,

44
00:03:14,889 --> 00:03:19,649
and merges it. It's an ordinary merge: if
you changed the vendored files yourself

45
00:03:19,689 --> 00:03:24,626
and upstream changed the same lines, you
resolve a normal content conflict in normal

46
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:24,982
files.

47
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:30,209
The --squash option has to be used
consistently. A repository whose subtree was

48
00:03:30,249 --> 00:03:35,073
added with --squash has none of the
upstream commits in its history, so a pull

49
00:03:35,113 --> 00:03:39,064
without it tries to merge two
histories with no common ancestor.

50
00:03:39,404 --> 00:03:44,292
Contributing back. Because the files are
yours, one commit can change the library

51
00:03:44,332 --> 00:03:47,760
and the application together,
which a submodule can't do.

52
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:52,870
🟢 SAFE: git subtree split walks your history
and synthesizes a history that contains

53
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:57,493
only what happened under the prefix,
with the prefix removed from the paths.

54
00:03:57,533 --> 00:04:01,204
git subtree push is a split
followed by a push of the result.

55
00:04:01,544 --> 00:04:05,103
The price. The repository grows
with every vendored version.

56
00:04:05,143 --> 00:04:10,140
The link to upstream lives in commit messages
that a rebase or a squash-merge of the

57
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:14,759
pull request can destroy. And everyone
who updates the copy must use the same

58
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:16,558
command with the same options.

59
00:04:16,898 --> 00:04:21,829
How to decide. The textbook's rule has four
steps. If the dependency is published as

60
00:04:21,869 --> 00:04:26,623
a package, use the package manager: it
solves versioning, transitive dependencies

61
00:04:26,663 --> 00:04:30,292
and security advisories, which
neither Git mechanism attempts.

62
00:04:30,332 --> 00:04:34,849
If it isn't a package and must be present
after a plain clone, use a subtree.

63
00:04:35,189 --> 00:04:39,970
If it must remain a separate repository,
with its own access rules, its own release

64
00:04:40,010 --> 00:04:44,501
cadence, or a size you don't want in your
history, and the team will adopt the

65
00:04:44,541 --> 00:04:49,089
recommended configuration, use a submodule.
And if the real wish is "one atomic

66
00:04:49,129 --> 00:04:52,981
commit across both", the two projects
may belong in one repository.

67
00:04:53,321 --> 00:04:58,118
When should you not use a subtree? When
the vendored content is large or changes

68
00:04:58,158 --> 00:05:01,202
often, because every version
stays in your history.

69
00:05:01,242 --> 00:05:05,797
Or when its access rules must differ
from those of the consuming repository.

70
00:05:07,637 --> 00:05:12,522
The textbook continues the recipe analogy.
Instead of a reference to page 212 of the

71
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:17,389
other book, you photocopy the page and bind
the copy into yours, with a note in the

72
00:05:17,429 --> 00:05:22,197
margin saying which printing it was copied
from. Every reader of your book has the

73
00:05:22,237 --> 00:05:22,607
recipe.

74
00:05:22,947 --> 00:05:27,429
The margin note is the only link to the
original. If somebody copies a newer

75
00:05:27,469 --> 00:05:32,248
printing over it without updating the note,
the next update has nothing to go by.

76
00:05:32,588 --> 00:05:36,711
That's where the analogy holds exactly,
and it's the thing to remember.

77
00:05:36,751 --> 00:05:41,402
A submodule's link is a tree entry that
Git reads. A subtree's link is text in a

78
00:05:41,442 --> 00:05:46,210
commit message that a script reads. Rewrite
or squash that commit, and the link is

79
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:47,558
gone, without an error.

80
00:05:47,898 --> 00:05:53,487
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Write
the entry for vendor/textsplit twice:

81
00:05:53,527 --> 00:05:58,005
once as a submodule, once as a
subtree. Predict the mode of each.

82
00:06:04,345 --> 00:06:09,275
Check your paper. On the left, one entry
of mode 160000 whose target is somewhere

83
00:06:09,315 --> 00:06:13,632
else. On the right, an ordinary tree,
mode 040000, with ordinary blobs.

84
00:06:13,672 --> 00:06:18,786
A plain clone of the left has an empty
directory. A plain clone of the right has the

85
00:06:18,826 --> 00:06:19,154
files.

86
00:06:23,494 --> 00:06:26,893
Predict how many new commits
appear. Say it out loud.

87
00:06:26,933 --> 00:06:27,542
I'll wait.

88
00:06:38,182 --> 00:06:44,202
Two. c77d389 is a commit with no parent in
your history, whose tree is the library's

89
00:06:44,242 --> 00:06:50,261
tree at upstream commit e216665. fb692e1
merges it into main and places that tree at

90
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:55,816
vendor/textsplit. Without --squash the
merge would bring the library's entire

91
00:06:55,856 --> 00:06:57,187
history into yours.

92
00:06:57,527 --> 00:07:01,868
Mode 040000, type tree.
Two ordinary files.

93
00:07:02,208 --> 00:07:05,302
And the message of the squash
commit, with the two trailers.

94
00:07:05,342 --> 00:07:07,183
That message is the whole mechanism.

95
00:07:07,523 --> 00:07:08,436
Now the teammate.

96
00:07:08,776 --> 00:07:14,459
A plain clone has the library. No init, no
update, no second fetch, no second set of

97
00:07:14,499 --> 00:07:20,046
credentials. Report 1 of the hook can't
happen. That's the claim from the opening,

98
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:20,387
kept.

99
00:07:20,727 --> 00:07:23,874
Asha has published textsplit 0.2.0.

100
00:07:31,214 --> 00:07:35,582
"Merge made by the 'ort' strategy": an
ordinary merge of a new squash commit.

101
00:07:35,922 --> 00:07:41,031
The new squash commit's message lists
the upstream commits it covers,

102
00:07:41,071 --> 00:07:44,763
e216665..4980f0b, and
records the new split point.

103
00:07:45,103 --> 00:07:51,073
A quick quiz. The same update, with --squash
forgotten: does Git merge it anyway, or

104
00:07:51,113 --> 00:07:52,790
refuse? Say it out loud.

105
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:02,551
It refuses: "refusing to merge
unrelated histories", exit status 128.

106
00:08:02,591 --> 00:08:06,217
Nothing was changed. Repeat
the pull with --squash.

107
00:08:06,257 --> 00:08:12,183
Better still, put the update command in a
script or an alias, so that everyone uses

108
00:08:12,223 --> 00:08:13,406
the same options.

109
00:08:13,746 --> 00:08:20,454
Back to the first replay. A change of
your own that touches the library and the

110
00:08:20,494 --> 00:08:22,675
application in one commit.

111
00:08:23,015 --> 00:08:28,943
One commit, e3827e6, two files:
ingest.py and the vendored splitter.py.

112
00:08:29,283 --> 00:08:31,293
Now offer the library part upstream.

113
00:08:31,633 --> 00:08:36,489
git subtree split will build a history
that contains only what happened under the

114
00:08:36,529 --> 00:08:41,505
prefix. Your repository was populated with
squash commits, so it has none of Asha's

115
00:08:41,545 --> 00:08:46,521
commits in its history. Predict whose
commits, and which IDs, the split branch will

116
00:08:46,561 --> 00:08:47,669
contain. I'll wait.

117
00:08:52,509 --> 00:08:58,623
Look at the IDs. The synthetic branch ends
in ee9c8f1: your commit reduced to its

118
00:08:58,663 --> 00:09:04,929
library part. ingest.py is gone from it.
Below it are 4980f0b, e216665 and ceaafe1:

119
00:09:04,969 --> 00:09:10,703
the upstream commits themselves, with
their original IDs and Asha as author.

120
00:09:10,743 --> 00:09:15,869
If you predicted new IDs all the
way down, that's the natural guess.

121
00:09:16,209 --> 00:09:19,412
The split found them through
the git-subtree-split trailers.

122
00:09:19,452 --> 00:09:23,843
The result is a branch that upstream can
merge or review as a normal contribution.

123
00:09:24,183 --> 00:09:27,634
A new branch in the library's
repository, at ee9c8f1.

124
00:09:27,674 --> 00:09:33,168
🟡 CAUTION: git subtree push creates or
updates a branch in the library's repository.

125
00:09:33,208 --> 00:09:35,738
Run split first and inspect the result.

126
00:09:48,078 --> 00:09:53,602
And for the teams that do choose submodules,
the configuration from section 23.16

127
00:09:53,642 --> 00:09:59,304
that removes most of the failures. It's
local configuration, so each clone and each

128
00:09:59,344 --> 00:10:00,403
CI job needs it.

129
00:10:06,243 --> 00:10:07,891
Five mistakes to watch for.

130
00:10:08,231 --> 00:10:13,228
Pulling a squashed subtree without --squash.
Root cause: the repository contains

131
00:10:13,268 --> 00:10:18,329
none of the upstream commits, so the pull
tries to merge histories with no common

132
00:10:18,369 --> 00:10:18,895
ancestor.

133
00:10:19,235 --> 00:10:23,849
Squash-merging or rebasing the pull
request that added or updated the subtree.

134
00:10:23,889 --> 00:10:28,801
Root cause: the link to upstream is two
trailers in a commit message; rewriting the

135
00:10:28,841 --> 00:10:30,770
commit discards or rewrites them.

136
00:10:31,110 --> 00:10:34,747
Vendoring a large or fast-changing
dependency as a subtree.

137
00:10:34,787 --> 00:10:38,549
Root cause: every version stays
in your repository's history.

138
00:10:38,889 --> 00:10:42,530
Using a submodule for a library
that is published as a package.

139
00:10:42,570 --> 00:10:47,147
Root cause: you rebuild a worse package
manager, without versioning, transitive

140
00:10:47,187 --> 00:10:48,725
dependencies or advisories.

141
00:10:49,065 --> 00:10:54,200
Running update --remote in CI for a submodule.
Root cause: the job tests a library

142
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:59,375
commit that no superproject commit records,
so the run cannot be reproduced; do it

143
00:10:59,415 --> 00:11:03,730
in a scheduled job that opens a pull
request with the pointer change.

144
00:11:05,570 --> 00:11:10,152
Now, out of the lab. A document-processing
team has three dependencies of three

145
00:11:10,192 --> 00:11:13,487
kinds, and after this chapter
it treats each differently.

146
00:11:13,827 --> 00:11:20,025
A tokenizer library that's published on a
package index: a version in the manifest

147
00:11:20,065 --> 00:11:26,416
and the lock file, nothing in Git beyond
that. A three-hundred-line chunking utility

148
00:11:26,456 --> 00:11:32,502
from an open-source project, which every
CI job and every Docker build must have

149
00:11:32,542 --> 00:11:38,816
after a plain clone and which changes twice
a year: a subtree, added with --squash,

150
00:11:38,856 --> 00:11:45,207
updated through one script so that the
options never vary, in pull requests that are

151
00:11:45,247 --> 00:11:49,087
merged with a merge commit
so the trailers survive.

152
00:11:49,427 --> 00:11:54,808
And a repository of protocol definitions
shared with seven other services, with its

153
00:11:54,848 --> 00:12:00,294
own access rules and release cadence: a
submodule, with the four configuration lines

154
00:12:00,334 --> 00:12:04,147
in the setup script that every
clone and every CI job runs.

155
00:12:04,487 --> 00:12:08,616
The decision record for each has one
sentence that begins "after a plain clone".

